{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Context and stores are usually the actual middle ground</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      If prop drilling is the pain point, the first thing to reach for isn't a bus, it's context (React) or an equivalent provide/inject mechanism, which still has one traceable place a value is set, it just skips passing it manually through components that don't use it. Only reach for a bus when you genuinely need "anyone, anywhere, can react to this," not as a generic fix for tedious prop passing.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A useful test before adding an event to a shared bus: can you name the one component that should own this state? If yes, it belongs in a store or context with a single write path, not a bus. If the honest answer is "lots of independent things care about this and none of them own it" (page-view analytics, a global toast queue, cross-tab logout sync), that's the actual bus use case.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Even for legitimate bus use cases, keep a typed event map (a discriminated union of event name → payload shape) so at minimum the compiler catches a payload mismatch, and consider a lightweight lint rule or code-search convention (a consistent `EVENT_` naming prefix, a checked-in registry of event names and their known emitters/listeners) so "who else touches this event" is discoverable without running the app. The debugging cost never fully goes away, but it can be bounded instead of open-ended.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

